.TH "VIRTUALBRICKS\-CONFIG" "5" "2026\-09\-28" "virtualbricks 2.1.0" "Virtualbricks Manual"
.SH NAME
virtualbricks\-config \- settings, state and project files of
Virtualbricks
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[I]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/settings.toml
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[I]$XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/state.toml
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/\f[I]project\f[R]/project.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
Virtualbricks keeps its configuration in three kinds of file, all
written in TOML 1.0:
.TP
\f[B]settings.toml\f[R]
Your preferences, which aren\(aqt about a project: the workspace, the
terminal, KSM and the tray icon.
.TP
\f[B]state.toml\f[R]
What Virtualbricks remembers between runs: the project that was open.
.TP
\f[B]project.toml\f[R]
One in each project: its bricks, events and disk images, how they are
connected, and its settings, such as the folders of QEMU and VDE.
.PP
Virtualbricks writes these files itself.
They can also be edited by hand, but never while Virtualbricks is
running: it rewrites \f[B]settings.toml\f[R] when it quits and the open
project\(aqs \f[B]project.toml\f[R] every three minutes, and your
changes would be lost.
.PP
The files of Virtualbricks 2.1 and older,
\f[I]\(ti/.virtualbricks.conf\f[R] and the \f[I].project\f[R] file of
each project, are converted once by the migration (see
\f[B]MIGRATION\f[R]).
Virtualbricks doesn\(aqt read them any more.
.SH COMMON RULES
.SS Format version
Every file starts with a \f[B]format\f[R] key, the version of its
layout, currently \f[B]1\f[R].
A file written by a newer Virtualbricks, with a higher \f[B]format\f[R],
is not read: its settings are not used and the file is never
overwritten, and a project in that format doesn\(aqt open.
A missing or invalid \f[B]format\f[R] is reported, and the file is read
as the current version.
.SS Every value is written
Virtualbricks writes every key, including the ones at their default
value, so a file shows the whole configuration.
When a default changes in a new version of Virtualbricks, existing files
keep their value.
.SS Reading is lenient
A file edited by hand doesn\(aqt have to be complete or tidy.
Each problem is reported in the messages window and in the log, with the
key it\(aqs about, and Virtualbricks carries on:
.IP \(bu 2
A missing key takes its default value.
.IP \(bu 2
A key with a value of the wrong type or out of range takes its default
value.
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown key is ignored, and dropped at the next save.
.IP \(bu 2
A brick of an unknown type, or with an invalid name, is dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
A connection to a socket that doesn\(aqt exist is left unconnected.
.PP
A file that isn\(aqt valid TOML is not read at all: Virtualbricks uses
the default settings and doesn\(aqt overwrite the file, and a project in
that state doesn\(aqt open.
.SS Writing
Virtualbricks writes a new file next to the old one and renames it over
the old one once it\(aqs complete, so a crash never leaves a
half\-written file.
It writes the whole file each time: comments, key order and formatting
of a hand edit are not kept.
.SS Names
Images, events and bricks are tables keyed by their name.
A name starts with a letter and contains only letters, digits,
underscores, hyphens and dots, and no two of them can share a name, even
of different kinds.
A name with a dot must be quoted in TOML, as in
\f[B][bricks.\(dqvm.1\(dq]\f[R].
.SS Types
The values have these types:
.TP
\f[I]boolean\f[R]
\f[B]true\f[R] or \f[B]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]integer\f[R], \f[I]number\f[R]
A whole number, or any number.
Some have a range, given as \f[I]min\f[R]\-\f[I]max\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]string\f[R], \f[I]path\f[R]
A string.
A path is the path of a file or a directory; an empty string means none.
.TP
\f[I]event\f[R], \f[I]image\f[R]
The name of an event or of a disk image of the project, or an empty
string for none.
.TP
\f[I]address\f[R]
An IPv4 address, as \f[B]\(dq10.0.0.1\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]choice\f[R]
One of the strings listed with the key.
.SH SETTINGS
The file \f[I]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/settings.toml, or
\f[I]\(ti/.config/virtualbricks/settings.toml\f[R] when
\f[B]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is not set.
Virtualbricks creates it with the default values at its first start, and
rewrites it when the settings window is closed with OK and when
Virtualbricks quits.
.TP
\f[B]workspace\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(ti/.virtualbricks\(dq\f[R]
The directory of the projects, \f[I].virtualbricks\f[R] in your home
directory by default.
Write it as an absolute path: \f[B]\(ti\f[R] is not expanded.
.TP
\f[B]term\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq/usr/bin/xterm\(dq\f[R]
The terminal that opens the console of a brick.
.TP
\f[B]ksm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Enable Kernel Samepage Merging at start, so that virtual machines share
identical memory pages.
.TP
\f[B]systray\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Show an icon in the system tray.
.TP
\f[B]show_missing\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Warn at start about the programs that Virtualbricks needs and can\(aqt
find, with the package that has each.
.TP
\f[B]audio_driver\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqalsa\(dq\f[R]
The audio driver of QEMU that plays the sound cards of the virtual
machines, as \f[B]\(dqalsa\(dq\f[R], \f[B]\(dqpa\(dq\f[R] or
\f[B]\(dqpipewire\(dq\f[R].
It\(aqs about this computer, not about a project.
A driver that the installed QEMU doesn\(aqt have leaves the machines
without a sound card, with a warning.
.PP
The \f[B]settings.toml\f[R] of an older version also has the settings of
the projects, described under \f[B]Project settings\f[R]: they\(aqre
reported, ignored, and dropped at the next save.
Each project has its own.
.SH STATE
The file \f[I]$XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/state.toml, or
\f[I]\(ti/.local/state/virtualbricks/state.toml\f[R] when
\f[B]XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R] is not set.
Virtualbricks rewrites it whenever it opens a project.
.TP
\f[B]current_project\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqnew_project\(dq\f[R]
The project that opens at start.
A missing \f[B]new_project\f[R] is created.
Any other project that can\(aqt be opened is reported, and Virtualbricks
creates and opens \f[B]new_project_0\f[R], or the next free number,
instead.
.SH PROJECTS
A project is a directory of the workspace that contains a
\f[B]project.toml\f[R].
The name of the directory is the name of the project, so renaming a
project renames its directory.
The directory also holds:
.TP
\f[I]README\f[R]
The description of the project: plain text, read as Markdown, see
\f[B]README\f[R] below.
.TP
\f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow
The private copy\-on\-write disk of a virtual machine, for example
\f[I]router1_hda.cow\f[R] (see \f[B]disks\f[R] under \f[B]qemu\f[R]).
.PP
The file has these top\-level keys:
.TP
\f[B]format\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
The version of the layout, see \f[B]COMMON RULES\f[R].
.TP
\f[B][settings]\f[R]
The settings of the project, see \f[B]Project settings\f[R].
.TP
\f[B][images.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R]
A disk image that virtual machines can use.
.TP
\f[B][events.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R]
An event: commands to run, after a delay.
.TP
\f[B][bricks.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R]
A brick.
Its \f[B]type\f[R] key says which kind; the other keys depend on it.
.SS Project settings
The \f[B][settings]\f[R] table.
While the project is open, these are the settings in effect, and the
settings window changes them for this project only.
A new project starts with a copy of the settings of the project that is
open, or with the defaults when none is.
An imported project brings the paths of the machine it comes from; the
import dialog offers to replace them with those of the open project.
.TP
\f[B]cowfmt\f[R] = \f[I]choice\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqqcow2\(dq\f[R]
The format of the private copy\-on\-write disks of the virtual machines:
\f[B]\(dqcow\(dq\f[R], \f[B]\(dqqcow\(dq\f[R] or
\f[B]\(dqqcow2\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]erroronloop\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Log an error when starting a brick finds a loop in the network.
.TP
\f[B]femaleplugs\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Allow plugs to connect to the socket cards of virtual machines, not only
to switches.
.TP
\f[B]qemupath\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq/usr/bin\(dq\f[R]
The directory of the QEMU programs.
.TP
\f[B]vdepath\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq/usr/bin\(dq\f[R]
The directory of the VDE programs, such as \f[B]vde_switch\f[R](1).
.SS Images
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The image file.
A relative path is relative to the project directory.
An image without a path is dropped; an image whose file is missing is
kept, and reported.
Two images can\(aqt use the same file.
.TP
\f[B]description\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
A description, which can span several lines.
.SS Events
.TP
\f[B]delay\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]0\f[R]
Seconds to wait before running the actions.
.TP
\f[B]actions\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R], default \f[CR][]\f[R]
The commands to run, in order.
Each one is an inline table with two keys: \f[B]kind\f[R], which is
\f[B]\(dqvb\(dq\f[R] for a command of the Virtualbricks console or
\f[B]\(dqshell\(dq\f[R] for a command run by \f[B]sh\f[R](1), and
\f[B]command\f[R], the command itself.
For example:
.RS
.IP
.EX
actions = [
    {kind = \(dqvb\(dq, command = \(dqsw1 on\(dq},
    {kind = \(dqshell\(dq, command = \(dqlogger lab started\(dq},
]
.EE
.RE
.SS Bricks
Every brick table has a \f[B]type\f[R], which is one of \f[B]qemu\f[R],
\f[B]switch\f[R], \f[B]switchwrapper\f[R], \f[B]tap\f[R],
\f[B]capture\f[R], \f[B]wire\f[R], \f[B]netemu\f[R],
\f[B]tunnellisten\f[R], \f[B]tunnelconnect\f[R] and \f[B]router\f[R],
and these two keys:
.TP
\f[B]pon_vbevent\f[R] = \f[I]event\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The event that runs when the brick starts.
.TP
\f[B]poff_vbevent\f[R] = \f[I]event\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The event that runs when the brick stops.
.PP
The other keys are described below for each type.
.SS Connections
A connection goes from a plug of a brick to a socket of another.
It\(aqs written in the brick that owns the plug, as a string that names
the socket:
.TP
\f[I]brick\f[R]
The only socket of a switch or a switch wrapper, for example
\f[B]\(dqsw1\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]vm\f[R]:\f[I]card\f[R]
A socket card of a virtual machine, for example
\f[B]\(dqrouter1:lan\(dq\f[R].
.PP
An empty string leaves the plug unconnected.
Depending on its type, a brick has one plug in \f[B]connect\f[R], two in
\f[B]endpoints\f[R], or network cards in \f[B]nics\f[R].
.SS README
The \f[I]README\f[R] of a project is plain text in UTF\-8, which
Virtualbricks reads as Markdown.
The Readme tab shows it rendered, and its pencil button edits the text;
the details of the Projects window show it rendered too.
The list of projects shows its first line, and the import window its
first paragraph that isn\(aqt a heading.
The syntax is a subset of CommonMark, the part that most editors and
forges share:
.TP
\f[B]# Title\f[R], \f[B]## Title\f[R]
A heading, with up to six \f[B]#\f[R].
A line underlined with \f[B]===\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-\-\f[R] is a heading
too.
.TP
\f[I]a new line\f[R]
A line break, so a README written as plain text keeps its lines.
An empty line starts a new paragraph.
.TP
\f[B]*italic*\f[R], \f[B]**bold**\f[R], \f[B]\(ti\(tistruck\(ti\(ti\f[R]
Emphasis.
.TP
\f[B]\(gacode\(ga\f[R]
Code.
A line of three backquotes, \f[B]\(ga\(ga\(ga\f[R], above and below some
lines makes a block of code.
.TP
\f[B]\- item\f[R], \f[B]1. item\f[R]
An item of a list.
An item indented under another is in a list inside it.
.TP
\f[B]> text\f[R]
A quote.
.TP
\f[B][text](https://...)\f[R], \f[B]<https://...>\f[R]
A link.
A URL written as it is, starting with \f[B]http://\f[R] or
\f[B]https://\f[R], is a link too.
Only \f[B]http\f[R], \f[B]https\f[R] and \f[B]mailto\f[R] links open, in
the browser or the mail client.
.TP
\f[B]![text](path)\f[R]
A picture, which shows as its text.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-\-\f[R]
A rule, between empty lines.
.TP
\f[B]\(rs\f[R]
A backslash before a mark keeps the mark: \f[B]\(rs*\f[R] is a star.
.PP
Anything else shows as it\(aqs written: a table, HTML, a line indented
by four spaces.
.SH BRICK TYPES
.SS qemu
A QEMU virtual machine.
Most keys become an option of the QEMU command line, given in bold.
Before a machine starts, Virtualbricks asks its QEMU program what it
has, and writes each option the way that version takes it.
What the program lacks is left out, and the machine starts with a
warning that says so, as a machine type or a CPU model that it
doesn\(aqt know, which leaves QEMU\(aqs default in its place.
.TP
\f[B]argv0\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqqemu\-system\-i386\(dq\f[R]
The QEMU program, in the \f[B]qemupath\f[R] directory, for example
\f[B]\(dqqemu\-system\-x86_64\(dq\f[R].
Empty means \f[B]qemu\-system\-x86_64\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]machine\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The machine type, as in \f[B]\-machine type=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]cpu\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The CPU model: \f[B]\-cpu\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]kvm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Use KVM when the host supports it: \f[B]\-accel kvm \-accel tcg\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]smp\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-64, default \f[CR]1\f[R]
The number of CPUs: \f[B]\-smp\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]ram\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-99999, default \f[CR]64\f[R]
The memory, in MiB: \f[B]\-m\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]kvmsm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Set the size of the KVM shadow memory to \f[B]kvmsmem\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]kvmsmem\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 0\-99999, default \f[CR]1\f[R]
The size of the KVM shadow memory, in MiB: \f[B]\-accel
kvm,kvm\-shadow\-mem=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]boot\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The boot order: \f[B]\-boot\f[R], for example \f[B]\(dqc\(dq\f[R] for
the first disk or \f[B]\(dqd\(dq\f[R] for the CD\-ROM.
Empty means the QEMU default.
.TP
\f[B]snapshot\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Write to temporary files instead of the disk images:
\f[B]\-snapshot\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]use_virtio\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Attach the disks as virtio devices.
.TP
\f[B]cdromen\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Use the image \f[B]cdrom\f[R] as the CD\-ROM.
.TP
\f[B]cdrom\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
An image file for the CD\-ROM: \f[B]\-cdrom\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]deviceen\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Use the host device \f[B]device\f[R] as the CD\-ROM, unless
\f[B]cdromen\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[B]device\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
A CD\-ROM device of the host, for example \f[B]\(dq/dev/cdrom\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]novga\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
No display: \f[B]\-display none\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]vga\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
A standard VGA card: \f[B]\-vga std\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]vnc\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Show the display over VNC, on display \f[B]vncN\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]vncN\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 0\-500, default \f[CR]1\f[R]
The VNC display: \f[B]\-vnc :\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]sdl\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Show the display in an SDL window: \f[B]\-display sdl\f[R].
QEMU has it with the package \f[B]qemu\-system\-gui\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]portrait\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Rotate the display: \f[B]\-portrait\f[R], which QEMU 8.2 has and 9.2
doesn\(aqt.
.TP
\f[B]soundhw\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The sound card, as \f[B]\(dqac97\(dq\f[R], \f[B]\(dqes1370\(dq\f[R] or
\f[B]\(dqsb16\(dq\f[R]: \f[B]\-audiodev\f[R], with the
\f[B]audio_driver\f[R] setting, and \f[B]\-device\f[R].
\f[B]\(dqpcspk\(dq\f[R] is the speaker of the PC: \f[B]\-machine
pcspk\-audiodev=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]usbmode\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Enable USB, \f[B]\-usb\f[R], and pass the devices of
\f[B]usbdevlist\f[R] to the guest.
.TP
\f[B]usbdevlist\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R], default \f[CR][]\f[R]
USB devices of the host, each an inline table with an \f[B]id\f[R], the
vendor and product id as \f[B]\(dq046d:c52b\(dq\f[R], and a
\f[B]description\f[R].
Each device becomes \f[B]\-device
usb\-host,vendorid=0x046d,productid=0xc52b\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]keyboard\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The keyboard layout, a code of two letters such as \f[B]\(dqit\(dq\f[R]:
\f[B]\-k\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]rtc\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Set the clock of the guest to the local time: \f[B]\-rtc
base=localtime\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]tdf\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Correct the drift of the clock: \f[B]\-rtc driftfix=slew\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]serial\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Connect the serial port to a socket in the runtime directory:
\f[B]\-serial unix:\f[R]...**_serial**.
.TP
\f[B]kernelenbl\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Boot the kernel \f[B]kernel\f[R] directly.
.TP
\f[B]kernel\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
A kernel image: \f[B]\-kernel\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]initrdenbl\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Use the initial ramdisk \f[B]initrd\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]initrd\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
An initial ramdisk: \f[B]\-initrd\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]kopt\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The kernel command line, with \f[B]kernelenbl\f[R]: \f[B]\-append\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]gdb\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Wait for a GDB connection on port \f[B]gdbport\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]gdbport\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-65535, default \f[CR]1234\f[R]
The port of the GDB server: \f[B]\-gdb tcp::\f[R]\f[I]port\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]noacpi\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
\f[B]\(dq*\(dq\f[R] disables ACPI: \f[B]\-machine acpi=off\f[R], or
\f[B]\-no\-acpi\f[R] for a machine type without that property.
.TP
\f[B]loadvm\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
A saved state to resume the machine from: \f[B]\-loadvm\f[R].
Virtualbricks sets it while it resumes a machine; leave it empty.
.TP
\f[B]icon\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
An image file that shows the machine in the main window.
.TP
\f[B]stdout\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
Not used.
.SS Disks
The disks are in the subtables \f[B]disks.hda\f[R], \f[B]disks.hdb\f[R],
\f[B]disks.hdc\f[R], \f[B]disks.hdd\f[R], \f[B]disks.fda\f[R],
\f[B]disks.fdb\f[R] and \f[B]disks.mtdblock\f[R], one for each device,
all of them always written:
.TP
\f[B]disks.\f[R]\f[I]device\f[R]\f[B].image\f[R] = \f[I]image\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The image of the device, by name.
.TP
\f[B]disks.\f[R]\f[I]device\f[R]\f[B].private\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Write to a private copy\-on\-write file,
\f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow in the project directory, instead of
the image, which then stays unchanged.
The format of the file is the project\(aqs \f[B]cowfmt\f[R].
.SS Network cards
\f[B]nics\f[R] is an array of tables, the network cards in the order the
guest sees them.
Each card has these keys:
.TP
\f[B]kind\f[R] = \f[I]choice\f[R]
\f[B]\(dqplug\(dq\f[R] for a card plugged into a socket,
\f[B]\(dqsocket\(dq\f[R] for a card that other bricks plug into, or
\f[B]\(dqhostonly\(dq\f[R] for a card on QEMU\(aqs user networking.
A plug and a socket card need the \f[B]vde\f[R] network backend of QEMU,
which Ubuntu builds its QEMU without: with a QEMU that lacks it, the
card is there but unplugged, with a warning.
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
With \f[B]\(dqplug\(dq\f[R], the socket the card is plugged into; see
\f[B]Connections\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
With \f[B]\(dqsocket\(dq\f[R], the name of the socket card, made of
letters, digits, underscores, hyphens and dots.
Other bricks connect to it as \f[I]vm\f[R]:\f[I]name\f[R].
When it\(aqs missing, the card is named \f[B]sock_eth\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R]
after its position.
.TP
\f[B]model\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqrtl8139\(dq\f[R]
The model of the card, for example \f[B]\(dqe1000\(dq\f[R] or
\f[B]\(dqvirtio\-net\-pci\(dq\f[R].
A model that the installed QEMU doesn\(aqt have becomes
\f[B]\(dqrtl8139\(dq\f[R], with a warning.
.TP
\f[B]mac\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The MAC address, as \f[B]\(dq52:54:00:12:34:56\(dq\f[R].
A missing or invalid address is replaced with a random one.
.SS switch
A VDE switch, \f[B]vde_switch\f[R](1).
Plugs connect to it by its name.
.TP
\f[B]numports\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-128, default \f[CR]32\f[R]
The number of ports.
.TP
\f[B]hub\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Send every packet to every port, like a hub.
.TP
\f[B]fstp\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]false\f[R]
Enable the fast spanning tree protocol.
.SS switchwrapper
A VDE switch that Virtualbricks doesn\(aqt start, run by another
program.
Plugs connect to it by its name.
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The control directory of the switch.
.SS tap
A tap interface of the host, plugged into a switch through
\f[B]vde_plug2tap\f[R](1).
It needs root, see \f[B]PRIVILEGES\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The socket the tap is plugged into.
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R] = \f[I]choice\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqoff\(dq\f[R]
How the interface gets its address: \f[B]\(dqoff\(dq\f[R] for not at
all, \f[B]\(dqdhcp\(dq\f[R], or \f[B]\(dqmanual\(dq\f[R] for
\f[B]ip\f[R], \f[B]nm\f[R] and \f[B]gw\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]ip\f[R] = \f[I]address\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq10.0.0.1\(dq\f[R]
The address of the interface.
.TP
\f[B]nm\f[R] = \f[I]address\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq255.255.255.0\(dq\f[R]
The netmask.
.TP
\f[B]gw\f[R] = \f[I]address\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The default gateway, or empty for none.
.SS capture
Captures the packets of an interface of the host into a switch, through
\f[B]vde_pcapplug\f[R].
It needs root, see \f[B]PRIVILEGES\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The socket the capture is plugged into.
.TP
\f[B]iface\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The interface of the host, for example \f[B]\(dqeth0\(dq\f[R].
.SS wire
Connects two sockets, through \f[B]dpipe\f[R](1) and
\f[B]vde_plug\f[R](1).
.TP
\f[B]endpoints\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R], default \f[CR][\(dq\(dq, \(dq\(dq]\f[R]
The two sockets, left and right.
.SS netemu
A wire that emulates a network link: bandwidth, delay, buffer and loss,
which can change over time between the states of a Markov chain.
It runs \f[B]vde\-netemu\f[R], or the \f[B]wirefilter\f[R](1) of VDE,
which it\(aqs a fork of, where vde\-netemu isn\(aqt installed.
.TP
\f[B]endpoints\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R], default \f[CR][\(dq\(dq, \(dq\(dq]\f[R]
The two sockets, left and right.
LR values apply from left to right, RL values from right to left.
.TP
\f[B]transperiod\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] >= 1, default \f[CR]100\f[R]
How often the emulator may change state, in milliseconds.
.TP
\f[B]transitions\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R], default \f[CR][[0.0]]\f[R]
The transition matrix: the number in row \f[I]i\f[R] and column
\f[I]j\f[R] is the probability of going from state \f[I]i\f[R] to state
\f[I]j\f[R] at each period.
It has a row and a column for each state.
.PP
\f[B]states\f[R] is an array of tables, at least one, and the first one
is the state the emulator starts in.
Each state has these keys:
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dqdefault name\(dq\f[R]
The name of the state.
.TP
\f[B]bandwidth\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]125000\f[R]
The bandwidth, in bytes per second, or 0 for no limit; from left to
right when \f[B]bandwidthsymm\f[R] is false.
.TP
\f[B]bandwidthr\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]125000\f[R]
The bandwidth from right to left.
.TP
\f[B]bandwidthsymm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Use \f[B]bandwidth\f[R] in both directions.
.TP
\f[B]delay\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]0\f[R]
The propagation delay, one way, in milliseconds; from left to right when
\f[B]delaysymm\f[R] is false.
.TP
\f[B]delayr\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]0\f[R]
The delay from right to left.
.TP
\f[B]delaysymm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Use \f[B]delay\f[R] in both directions.
.TP
\f[B]chanbufsize\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]75000\f[R]
The size of the channel buffer, in bytes, or 0 for no limit; from left
to right when \f[B]chanbufsizesymm\f[R] is false.
.TP
\f[B]chanbufsizer\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R], default \f[CR]75000\f[R]
The size of the channel buffer from right to left.
.TP
\f[B]chanbufsizesymm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Use \f[B]chanbufsize\f[R] in both directions.
.TP
\f[B]loss\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] 0\-100, default \f[CR]0.0\f[R]
The percentage of packets lost; from left to right when
\f[B]losssymm\f[R] is false.
.TP
\f[B]lossr\f[R] = \f[I]number\f[R] 0\-100, default \f[CR]0.0\f[R]
The percentage of packets lost from right to left.
.TP
\f[B]losssymm\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R], default \f[CR]true\f[R]
Use \f[B]loss\f[R] in both directions.
.SS tunnellisten
The server end of an encrypted tunnel between two machines, through
\f[B]vde_cryptcab\f[R](1).
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The socket the tunnel is plugged into.
.TP
\f[B]port\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-65535, default \f[CR]7667\f[R]
The UDP port to listen on.
.TP
\f[B]password\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The password of the tunnel.
It\(aqs stored in clear text.
.SS tunnelconnect
The client end of an encrypted tunnel, through
\f[B]vde_cryptcab\f[R](1).
.TP
\f[B]connect\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The socket the tunnel is plugged into.
.TP
\f[B]host\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The host that runs the server end.
.TP
\f[B]port\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-65535, default \f[CR]7667\f[R]
The UDP port of the server end.
.TP
\f[B]localport\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R] 1\-65535, default \f[CR]10771\f[R]
The local UDP port.
.TP
\f[B]password\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R], default \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The password of the tunnel.
It\(aqs stored in clear text.
.SS router
A router.
It has only the two event keys.
.SH PRIVILEGES
A \f[B]tap\f[R] and a \f[B]capture\f[R] open interfaces of the host, and
the \f[B]ksm\f[R] setting writes to \f[I]/sys\f[R]: they need root.
When Virtualbricks doesn\(aqt run as root, it runs them with
\f[B]sudo\f[R](8), in one of two ways:
.TP
\f[B]sudo \-A\f[R]
When an askpass helper is configured: the program of
\f[B]SUDO_ASKPASS\f[R], or of a \f[B]Path askpass\f[R] line of
\f[I]/etc/sudo.conf\f[R], as \f[B]ssh\-askpass\f[R](1).
sudo runs it to ask for your password in a window, so it needs a
display.
.TP
\f[B]sudo \-n\f[R]
Otherwise.
sudo never asks for a password: when it would have to, it fails at once,
and the brick doesn\(aqt start.
This is the way on a machine without a display, with a rule of
\f[B]sudoers\f[R](5) that lets you run the programs of the bricks, from
the folder of the \f[B]vdepath\f[R] setting, without a password:
.RS
.IP
.EX
alice ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/vde_plug2tap, \(rs
    /usr/bin/vde_pcapplug
.EE
.RE
.PP
The \f[B]ksm\f[R] setting runs \f[I]/bin/sh\f[R] as root to write its
value, and a rule that lets a shell run as root gives root to anyone who
can use it.
Turn KSM on at boot instead, with a \f[B]tmpfiles.d\f[R](5) line, and
leave \f[B]ksm\f[R] false:
.IP
.EX
w /sys/kernel/mm/ksm/run \- \- \- \- 1
.EE
.PP
The settings of older versions have a \f[B]sudo\f[R] key, the program
that did this: it\(aqs reported, ignored, and dropped at the next save.
.SH MIGRATION
The first time Virtualbricks starts after an upgrade from 2.1 or older,
it converts \f[I]\(ti/.virtualbricks.conf\f[R] into
\f[B]settings.toml\f[R] and \f[B]state.toml\f[R], and the
\f[I].project\f[R] file of each project into a \f[B]project.toml\f[R]
next to it, and shows what it converted in a window.
The settings of \f[I]\(ti/.virtualbricks.conf\f[R] that are now a
project\(aqs, as \f[B]qemupath\f[R], go into each converted project.
The old files are left as they were.
.PP
The same conversion can be tried on copies, into a new folder laid out
like the XDG directories:
.IP
.EX
python \-m virtualbricks.migrate \-\-settings \(ti/.virtualbricks.conf \(rs
    \(ti/vb\-copy /tmp/vb\-test
.EE
.PP
\f[B]virtualbricks\-migrate\f[R] opens the same conversion in a window,
and \f[B]python \-m virtualbricks.migrate \-\-help\f[R] lists every
option.
An archive of an old project is converted when it\(aqs imported.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/settings.toml
The settings.
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/state.toml
The state.
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/migration\-report.txt
The report of the last migration.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/\f[I]project\f[R]/project.toml
A project.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/vimages/
The images saved when a project is imported.
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]/virtualbricks/\f[I]project\f[R]/
The sockets and consoles of the running bricks, removed when you log
out.
.TP
\f[I]/tmp/vb.lock\f[R]
The lock that lets only one Virtualbricks run on the machine.
.TP
\f[I]/etc/sudo.conf\f[R]
Its \f[B]Path askpass\f[R] line makes Virtualbricks run sudo with
\f[B]\-A\f[R], as \f[B]SUDO_ASKPASS\f[R] does; see \f[B]PRIVILEGES\f[R].
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R], \f[B]XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]
Where the settings and the state are, \f[I]\(ti/.config\f[R] and
\f[I]\(ti/.local/state\f[R] when they are not set.
Relative paths are ignored, as the XDG Base Directory specification
says.
.TP
\f[B]XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]
Where the sockets are.
When it\(aqs not set, they are in a directory
\f[I]virtualbricks\-\f[R]uid of the temporary directory, readable only
by you.
.TP
\f[B]SUDO_ASKPASS\f[R]
The askpass helper of \f[B]sudo\f[R](8): when it\(aqs set, Virtualbricks
runs sudo with \f[B]\-A\f[R]; see \f[B]PRIVILEGES\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
A \f[B]settings.toml\f[R] as Virtualbricks writes it at its first start:
.IP
.EX
format = 1
workspace = \(dq/home/alice/.virtualbricks\(dq
term = \(dq/usr/bin/xterm\(dq
ksm = false
systray = true
show_missing = true
audio_driver = \(dqalsa\(dq
.EE
.PP
A \f[B]project.toml\f[R] with a virtual machine, two switches connected
by a network emulator with two states, a tap and an event.
The virtual machine is shortened: Virtualbricks writes all of its keys,
and the six empty disks.
.IP
.EX
format = 1

[settings]
cowfmt = \(dqqcow2\(dq
erroronloop = false
femaleplugs = false
qemupath = \(dq/usr/bin\(dq
vdepath = \(dq/usr/bin\(dq

[images.debian]
path = \(dq/home/alice/.virtualbricks/vimages/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq
description = \(dqDebian 12\(rsnbase image\(dq

[events.start_lab]
actions = [
    {kind = \(dqvb\(dq, command = \(dqsw1 on\(dq},
    {kind = \(dqshell\(dq, command = \(dqlogger lab started\(dq},
]
delay = 5

[bricks.sw1]
type = \(dqswitch\(dq
pon_vbevent = \(dqstart_lab\(dq
poff_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
numports = 16
hub = false
fstp = true

[bricks.sw2]
type = \(dqswitch\(dq
pon_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
poff_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
numports = 32
hub = false
fstp = false

[bricks.router1]
type = \(dqqemu\(dq
pon_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
poff_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
argv0 = \(dqqemu\-system\-x86_64\(dq
kvm = true
smp = 2
ram = 1024
# ... and the other keys of a qemu brick

[bricks.router1.disks.hda]
image = \(dqdebian\(dq
private = true

# ... and hdb to mtdblock, with image = \(dq\(dq and private = false

[[bricks.router1.nics]]
kind = \(dqplug\(dq
connect = \(dqsw1\(dq
model = \(dqe1000\(dq
mac = \(dq52:54:00:12:34:01\(dq

[[bricks.router1.nics]]
kind = \(dqhostonly\(dq
model = \(dqvirtio\-net\-pci\(dq
mac = \(dq52:54:00:12:34:02\(dq

[[bricks.router1.nics]]
kind = \(dqsocket\(dq
name = \(dqlan\(dq
model = \(dqe1000\(dq
mac = \(dq52:54:00:12:34:03\(dq

[bricks.tap0]
type = \(dqtap\(dq
pon_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
poff_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
ip = \(dq10.0.0.1\(dq
nm = \(dq255.255.255.0\(dq
gw = \(dq\(dq
mode = \(dqmanual\(dq
connect = \(dqsw2\(dq

[bricks.wan]
type = \(dqnetemu\(dq
pon_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
poff_vbevent = \(dq\(dq
transperiod = 100
transitions = [[0.0, 0.2], [0.5, 0.0]]
endpoints = [\(dqsw1\(dq, \(dqsw2\(dq]

[[bricks.wan.states]]
name = \(dqgood\(dq
bandwidth = 125000
bandwidthr = 125000
bandwidthsymm = true
delay = 10
delayr = 0
delaysymm = true
chanbufsize = 75000
chanbufsizer = 75000
chanbufsizesymm = true
loss = 0.0
lossr = 0.0
losssymm = true

[[bricks.wan.states]]
name = \(dqcongested\(dq
bandwidth = 125000
bandwidthr = 125000
bandwidthsymm = true
delay = 200
delayr = 50
delaysymm = false
chanbufsize = 75000
chanbufsizer = 75000
chanbufsizesymm = true
loss = 2.5
lossr = 0.0
losssymm = true
.EE
.SH BUGS
The \f[B]mode\f[R], \f[B]ip\f[R], \f[B]nm\f[R] and \f[B]gw\f[R] of a tap
are stored but not applied: the interface gets no address from
Virtualbricks.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]virtualbricks\-archive\f[R](7), \f[B]qemu\f[R](1),
\f[B]vde_switch\f[R](1), \f[B]vde_plug2tap\f[R](1),
\f[B]vde_cryptcab\f[R](1), \f[B]dpipe\f[R](1), \f[B]sudo\f[R](8),
\f[B]sudoers\f[R](5), \f[B]sudo.conf\f[R](5), \f[B]tmpfiles.d\f[R](5)
.PP
